From 25613dac9f7747f02b8e96ab0ae4c37e84017e37 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Thomas Gambier <thomas.gambier@nexedi.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 11:45:28 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Add IZODBStreamIterator to stream data from the ZODB

The WSGI publisher closes the ZODB connection of the request before the
WSGI server iterates on the response body. So an IStreamIterator must
not load objects from the ZODB, and an application that sends big data
from the ZODB must first load all of it in memory.

A response body that provides the new IZODBStreamIterator interface can
load objects from the ZODB while the WSGI server iterates on it. The
publisher keeps the load_app context, and with it the ZODB connection of
the request, open until the WSGI server calls close on the returned
iterable. publish_module uses an ExitStack for this, so this also works
when load_app is replaced, for example by Testing.ZopeTestCase. The
ExitStack closes the body first, and then exits the load_app context.

If an error occurs after the publication, for example in request.close
or in start_response, the publisher closes the body and the connection.
For a HEAD request, the publisher closes the body at once and sends an
empty body. The publisher does not give such a body to
wsgi.file_wrapper, because the WSGI server can read a file wrapper in
another thread.

The WSGI server can close the iterable in a thread that is not the
thread of the publication. So load_app aborts the transaction with the
transaction manager of the thread that opened the application, not with
the transaction manager of the current thread.

The publish method of Testing.ZopeTestCase.FunctionalTestCase and the
http function of the functional doctests read all the WSGI result, and
then close it like a WSGI server. Otherwise, an IZODBStreamIterator
would keep its ZODB connection open.

The tests give a dummy application to the real load_app. The dummy
application records when load_app opens it and when load_app closes its
ZODB connection.

